/*
some advice when using buildSpriteTile:
In basic terms, Treat each instr you give it like a paint-by-numbers thing.
use the colors map to translate between numbers and colors, give it a blank tile
and the result will be a sprite tile, ready for use, fully colored.
When building an instr, make sure you assign at least one int-Pixel Pair
for transparency ( you can make a pixel transparent by setting the _isTransparent property of a Pixel Object to true)
(examplePixel._isTransparent = true;) AND ASSIGN IT TO ID 0
*/
Tile& buildSpriteTile(Tile& blankTile, std::vector<std::vector<int>> instr, std::map<int, Pixel> colors)
{
	if (instr.size() == 8) // a tile is 8x8 pixels. 8*8 = 64.
	{
		for (int i = 0; i < instr.size(); i++)
		{
			for (int j = 0; j < instr[i].size(); j++)
			{
				if (colors.count(instr[i][j]) != 0)
				{
					blankTile.setPixel(Pos(j, i), colors.at(instr[i][j]));
				}

			}
		}
	}
	return blankTile;
}

/*
QUICK-REFERENCE GUIDE:
Pos(int X,int Y) - A 2D, integer coordinate system
unit conversions:
-Static tilesToPixels() and pixelsToTiles()
-Operator- returns a struct called distances with int XDiff, int Ydiff and Euclidean distance.
-m_x and m_y: coordinate components
---------------------------------
Pixel(int R, int G, int B,Pos)
Inherits from Pos, has full 8-bit per channel color
-Change color - setNewRGB(R,G,B);
-compute transparency - trueAlpha(Pixel other, float alpha) Note: alpha should satisfy (alpha >=0.0 && alpha <= 1.0)
returns a struct called color, with properties int Red, int Green, int Blue, float transparency
-getRed(),getGreen(),getBlue() return each channel.
----------------------------------
Tile(Pos GPos, bool isSprite, bool collidable = false)
an 8x8 collection of Pixel objects.
-void setPixel(Pos, Pixel) and Pixel getPixel(Pos) -> manipulates a tile's pixels
-Pos getGlobalPos(), bool getIsSprite(), std::vector<std::vector<Pixel>> getTileData() ->useful getters
-setNewGlobalPos() -> Don't use this if getIsSprite() returns true (it won't do anything). Sets the Tile's global position.

Please, read the documentation in the Tile class.
I advise against messing around with tiles before reading the tile class definition fully.
sincerely, the guy who made this code.
-----------------------------------
Sprite()
addTile(Tile, int),editTile(int X ,int Y,Pos,Pixel),getTile(int X, int Y),replaceTile(int X,int Y,tile) -> useful tile editing functions
-updateGlobalPos(Pos); Move A Sprite
-getGlobalPos(); Where is a sprite
---------------------------------
Screen
WARNING! There should be exactly ONE (1) instance of Screen! A Screen is memory intensive, taking up no less than 1.15MB per instance.
(at draw time that minimum can go up to 4.7MB).
-setTile(Pos,Tile) getTile(Pos)
when it's time to draw a frame, call the following IN ORDER!
1) setNewScrollOffset(Pos) (OPTIONAL, but call before the end of game logic)
2) compositeSprite(Sprite) (Call for every Sprite, IndexedSprite and Entity in the loaded area!)
3) drawScreen(); <- YOU SHOULD ONLY CALL THIS ONCE!
4) resetCompositing();
--------------------------------
IndexedSprite() inherits from Sprite
-getCurrentFrame()
-setCurrentFrame(vector<Pos>)
-setupFrame() <- this is what you should call once per frame of animation.
---------------------------------
Entity(int HP) inherits from IndexedSprite.
-setHP(int HP);
-getHP();
-setCollision(bool); Entity instances are collidable by default!
-bool checkCollision(Tile) slower, more thorough collision check
-Pos calcPhysics(Pos gravVector, std::map<Pos,Tile>) attempts to move an entity in the direction, given. returns the new position if it succeeds
returns the current position if it can't.
-addAnimation(std::vector<std::vector<Pos>>)
-editAnimation(int, std::vector<std::vector<Pos>>)
-setCurrentAnimIndex(int)
-advanceOneFrame() <- Call This
